      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "THE PROBLEM",
          "content": "63-year-old man with a painful fibrous ankylosis of the left ankle in spite of two surgical attempts at arthrodesis. The subtalar joint had been fused 3 years previously during a reconstruction of the posterior tibial tendon. The severe, activity-related pain was partially due to a reflex sympathetic dystrophy. Wearing of orthopedic shoes did not relieve the symptoms; a below-knee amputation had been considered."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "THE SOLUTION",
          "content": "Tibiotalar arthrodesis in functional position with a free pedicled iliac bone graft."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SURGICAL TECHNIQUE",
          "content": "Microsurgical harvesting of a pedicled iliac bone graft. Debridement of the fibrous ankylosis and transplantation of the bone graft into an anterior tibiotalar groove."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULT",
          "content": "Bony fusion within 3 months. Pain-free weight bearing. The favorable outcome persisted after 1 year."
        }
      ]},
